1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,350
Hello.

2
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:37,670
Can everyone see my screen?

3
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:43,337
In the previous sharing sessions, Gioi and Huy showed how to perform a GTest UT task.

4
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:54,184
Those were manual methods.

5
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:03,374
The idea is to use a tool that automatically generates GTest test cases and saves effort.

6
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:19,974
The tool developed by our team is called UT AI Copilot, or AI Copilot for short.

7
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:30,377
Today, I will explain how to install the tool and what prerequisites are required.

8
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,400
I will also demonstrate how to use it on a UT task.

9
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,387
First, let us install the tool.

10
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:51,134
The tool is packaged as a ZIP file.

11
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:00,954
The installation steps are shown on this slide.

12
00:02:06,666 --> 00:02:08,600
First, download the ZIP file.

13
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:11,467
Then create the required folder on drive C.

14
00:02:17,446 --> 00:02:20,380
This path must be identical on all machines.

15
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:24,647
Create a folder named UTAI Code Workspace, extract the ZIP file.

16
00:02:37,003 --> 00:02:39,270
Copy the UTAI Code folder into it.

17
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:42,737
The tool is implemented as an extension for VS Code.

18
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:03,167
The ZIP filename includes the tool name and its release version.

19
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:11,360
The current version is 1.2.11.

20
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,160
The archive contains the UTAI Code folder.

21
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:29,380
Place it in the required workspace path.

22
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:30,380
Open VS Code.

23
00:03:44,113 --> 00:03:49,580
I will uninstall the version already installed on my machine and install it again.

24
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:54,180
Open Extensions, select the three-dot menu, choose Install from VSIX.

25
00:04:23,553 --> 00:04:26,220
Select the VSIX file for version 1.2.11.

26
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:31,687
Click Install and reload the workspace after the Complete Install message appears.

27
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:01,974
After reloading, press Ctrl+Shift+P, search for UTAI Code, and run UTAI Code: Open Panel.

28
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:13,444
When this interface appears, the extension has been installed successfully in VS Code.

29
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:20,680
Do you have any questions about installation?

30
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,680
Is this tool only for internal Bot use?

31
00:05:47,516 --> 00:05:51,650
Could installing an external extension violate a company rule?

32
00:05:51,650 --> 00:05:54,050
The tool belongs to the Bot project.

33
00:06:15,730 --> 00:06:20,530
Please contact Tien for more detailed guidance on deployment and policy.

34
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:24,797
We will discuss the AI, API, and security-related details later.

35
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:10,550
After installation, we will use the tool in a normal GTest workspace.

36
00:07:44,650 --> 00:07:48,650
Drag the C file onto the batch file to create the workspace.

37
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:51,250
Any objects shown in red are undefined.

38
00:08:16,273 --> 00:08:19,940
Define all of them before you start writing test cases.

39
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:24,607
The tool can search for these definitions and add them to the .h file.

40
00:08:31,970 --> 00:08:35,504
To use this feature, you need the task database path.

41
00:09:06,673 --> 00:09:10,540
The database contains related C, header, and source files.

42
00:09:10,540 --> 00:09:15,740
Open the tool, enter the module path, and then enter the database folder path.

43
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:50,337
Before performing UT, configure the preprocessor switches.

44
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:38,640
They determine which code blocks are enabled or disabled for testing.

45
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:42,440
Next, build the project from Terminal > Run Task > Build.

46
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:50,640
The build log displays errors.

47
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:03,140
Use Read Undefined Object from Build Log to read the unresolved objects.

48
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:07,507
The tool lists the undefined objects it finds.

49
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:32,334
Use the auto-find feature to retrieve their definitions from the database.

50
00:11:52,226 --> 00:11:54,160
Build and view the log again.

51
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:59,627
If an object is still missing, use auto-find for that object and then build again.

52
00:12:09,010 --> 00:12:14,210
If no more undefined objects can be read, the project may still fail to build.

53
00:12:41,056 --> 00:12:44,590
In that case, inspect and resolve the error manually.

54
00:12:44,590 --> 00:12:48,390
For example, the error may show that a struct is missing.

55
00:13:21,050 --> 00:13:26,384
Open the database in VS Code, search the header files for the struct definition.

56
00:14:21,750 --> 00:14:25,950
Copy it into the .h file, run the undefined-object check again.

57
00:14:25,950 --> 00:14:29,884
Objects not found in the database must be defined manually.

58
00:15:11,590 --> 00:15:15,190
The same applies to objects not supported by the tool.

59
00:15:15,190 --> 00:15:17,990
Define the remaining objects and run View.

60
00:15:52,956 --> 00:15:55,690
All undefined objects have been resolved.

61
00:15:55,690 --> 00:15:59,090
The next step is to define mock and stub functions.

62
00:16:08,255 --> 00:16:12,855
The tool helps collect undefined objects and add them to the .h file.

63
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:25,220
Unsupported cases still require manual handling.

64
00:16:25,220 --> 00:16:29,220
This saves considerable time searching through the database.

65
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:38,747
Do you have any questions about finding undefined objects?

66
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:46,040
Hello?

67
00:17:01,003 --> 00:17:02,070
Can you hear me?

68
00:17:02,070 --> 00:17:05,004
What form should the selected database take?

69
00:18:45,236 --> 00:18:48,570
Where does the database in this example come from?

70
00:18:48,570 --> 00:18:50,704
The demo uses a sample database.

71
00:19:05,012 --> 00:19:07,146
Each task has a database folder.

72
00:19:21,454 --> 00:19:25,588
Each task database folder contains related C and header files.

73
00:19:39,896 --> 00:19:43,830
The tool reads these headers and adds matching definitions.

74
00:19:43,830 --> 00:19:49,764
Do we need to choose the database folder ourselves, and is there a rule for selecting it?

75
00:20:26,430 --> 00:20:31,297
Normally, the task coordinator provides the C file and a database folder.

76
00:20:55,466 --> 00:20:58,534
Enter the location of that folder in the tool.

77
00:21:22,703 --> 00:21:25,770
There is no required physical location for it.

78
00:21:25,770 --> 00:21:29,704
You can provide a parent folder and let the tool search it.

79
00:21:49,477 --> 00:21:52,877
However, search speed depends on the database size.

80
00:22:12,650 --> 00:22:16,450
Choose the smallest folder that contains sufficient data.

81
00:22:16,450 --> 00:22:17,450
Thank you.

82
00:22:24,170 --> 00:22:26,704
The C file is no longer marked in red.

83
00:23:10,472 --> 00:23:15,072
The tool can also create mock and stub declarations in the .cpp file.

84
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:03,640
This includes functions under test, global variables, and mock bindings.

85
00:24:03,640 --> 00:24:09,240
One message-related object is a special case that cannot be generated automatically.

86
00:24:55,970 --> 00:25:00,770
Check its definition in the database and add it manually to the .h file.

87
00:25:00,770 --> 00:25:05,237
After cleaning and building again, one test case runs successfully.

88
00:25:37,103 --> 00:25:39,570
This completes the preparation stage.

89
00:25:39,570 --> 00:25:41,437
Delete the sample test case.

90
00:26:11,426 --> 00:26:14,960
The preparation features work without GitHub Copilot.

91
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:20,227
Automatic test-case generation requires GitHub Copilot and GitHub Copilot Chat.

92
00:27:07,566 --> 00:27:12,500
Install both extensions, log in, and verify that Copilot Chat can respond.

93
00:27:12,500 --> 00:27:17,100
Select the function to test and start automatic test-case generation.

94
00:28:39,910 --> 00:28:44,310
After selecting Auto Generate Test Case, wait for the tool to run.

95
00:29:01,766 --> 00:29:05,300
Do not click anywhere else while the tool is running.

96
00:29:05,300 --> 00:29:08,500
GitHub Copilot does not expose the required API.

97
00:29:20,203 --> 00:29:25,470
The tool uses focused copy-and-paste actions to exchange prompts and responses.

98
00:29:25,470 --> 00:29:28,937
You may use the scroll wheel to observe the process.

99
00:29:49,243 --> 00:29:51,910
Do not move focus by clicking elsewhere.

100
00:29:51,910 --> 00:29:57,310
The first prompt sends source code and definitions to Copilot for MCDC scenarios.

101
00:30:41,790 --> 00:30:46,724
It instructs Copilot not to assign local variables directly in test cases.

102
00:31:00,470 --> 00:31:05,204
The next prompt gives the .cpp format and requests matching test cases.

103
00:31:38,580 --> 00:31:44,314
Copilot generated eight scenarios, but its first response created only two test cases.

104
00:32:27,970 --> 00:32:30,237
The tool asks Copilot to continue.

105
00:32:50,718 --> 00:32:54,852
When the response is complete, the tool updates the test file.

106
00:33:15,333 --> 00:33:19,600
It inserts the test names and scenario descriptions as comments.

107
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:24,467
Run Cantata++ coverage and confirm that the selected function is covered.

108
00:34:18,690 --> 00:34:21,290
Next, we will choose a longer function.

109
00:37:37,766 --> 00:37:42,700
We will use a longer function to demonstrate the tool and its limitations.

110
00:37:42,700 --> 00:37:45,767
The first generated test cases may be correct.

111
00:38:14,593 --> 00:38:19,260
Copilot can still assign a local variable directly despite the prompt.

112
00:38:19,260 --> 00:38:25,194
In that case, inspect the code flow, determine how the local variable receives its value.

113
00:40:24,106 --> 00:40:26,640
Edit the generated test case manually.

114
00:40:26,640 --> 00:40:32,440
A local static variable cannot be controlled directly in a test case or through a stub.

115
00:41:11,183 --> 00:41:13,450
Remove invalid direct assignments.

116
00:41:13,450 --> 00:41:17,317
After running coverage, any red areas are still uncovered.

117
00:42:00,060 --> 00:42:04,260
Use Read Remaining Test Case to identify cases for those paths.

118
00:42:04,260 --> 00:42:09,460
The tool lists the missing coverage, such as the true branch of condition 360.

119
00:42:36,693 --> 00:42:40,160
The tool does the same for the remaining conditions.

120
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:45,294
If the table specifies a true or false branch, the result was read correctly.

121
00:43:07,443 --> 00:43:12,510
Delete rows shown as Undefined because the tool cannot generate those cases.

122
00:43:12,510 --> 00:43:16,777
Read Remaining Test Case uses the final test case as an example.

123
00:43:41,790 --> 00:43:46,390
Add detailed guidance to that example to improve the next generation.

124
00:43:46,390 --> 00:43:49,924
For example, use a value that avoids an early return.

125
00:44:23,936 --> 00:44:26,470
Add a comment explaining that purpose.

126
00:44:26,470 --> 00:44:29,004
Use the relevant value-setting fields.

127
00:44:49,190 --> 00:44:53,390
Read the remaining cases again so the tool reloads the example.

128
00:44:53,390 --> 00:44:58,990
Click Create More Test Case and wait for the tool to generate additional test cases.

129
00:46:22,520 --> 00:46:28,120
The tool sends the table information as a prompt and inserts corresponding comments.

130
00:46:28,120 --> 00:46:31,720
After the tool generates test cases, check the result.

131
00:47:55,066 --> 00:47:59,200
Check whether each one meets the requested coverage objective.

132
00:47:59,200 --> 00:48:05,000
Correct generated values when a case takes the wrong branch or does not avoid a return.

133
00:50:34,630 --> 00:50:40,297
For a simple function, the tool may generate eight test cases in about three minutes.

134
00:51:13,160 --> 00:51:15,360
This can save significant effort.

135
00:51:15,360 --> 00:51:20,894
For functions with nested conditions and return statements, errors are more likely.

136
00:51:37,780 --> 00:51:43,580
AI output may be inaccurate for functions with nested conditions and return statements.

137
00:51:43,580 --> 00:51:49,047
Read Remaining Case identifies uncovered cases and the route needed to reach them.

138
00:52:19,880 --> 00:52:23,947
For example, it can identify the required condition sequence.

139
00:53:11,533 --> 00:53:17,400
It can identify the sequence of true and false conditions needed to cover a target line.

140
00:53:17,400 --> 00:53:20,267
However, user validation is still required.

141
00:54:13,556 --> 00:54:19,290
Validate the result in Cantata and correct any case that misses its intended coverage.

142
00:54:19,290 --> 00:54:22,357
Can users obtain a Copilot account themselves?

143
00:54:42,420 --> 00:54:45,554
Internal users may follow the internal process.

144
00:55:05,616 --> 00:55:09,150
External users should contact the responsible person.

145
00:55:29,213 --> 00:55:31,880
Account details will be explained later.

146
00:55:31,880 --> 00:55:35,080
Clear Table only removes the data in this table.

147
00:56:00,990 --> 00:56:04,524
The final feature is used after building the project.

148
00:56:53,095 --> 00:56:58,162
To demonstrate it, we will intentionally create an incorrect expected value.

149
00:57:46,733 --> 00:57:51,800
We will make an expected value incorrect and observe the failure in the log.

150
00:57:51,800 --> 00:57:55,334
Use Fix Wrong Expected to correct the expected value.

151
00:58:14,233 --> 00:58:16,900
After rebuilding, the test passes again.

152
00:58:20,750 --> 00:58:23,950
This feature makes expected-value checks faster.

153
00:59:04,280 --> 00:59:07,680
Always investigate a difference before changing it.

154
00:59:07,680 --> 00:59:12,480
A different actual value may indicate a bug or an incorrect test design.

155
01:00:44,733 --> 01:00:47,400
It is not merely a wrong expected value.

156
01:00:47,400 --> 01:00:53,200
The feature can also restore missing expected calls or remove redundant expected calls.

157
01:03:02,936 --> 01:03:05,670
This reduces related warnings in the log.

158
01:03:05,670 --> 01:03:07,537
That concludes the training.

159
01:03:31,712 --> 01:03:34,312
The documents and files will be shared.

160
01:03:58,486 --> 01:04:02,420
You can contact me to report any issue when using the tool.

161
01:04:02,420 --> 01:04:04,754
Now Tien will explain the accounts.

162
01:04:18,629 --> 01:04:22,363
The move from Cantata to GTest created a heavy workload.

163
01:04:36,237 --> 01:04:40,971
The team developed automation tools as AI and Copilot became available.

164
01:04:54,846 --> 01:05:00,780
The tool is still new and needs updates, but it already automates many tester activities.

165
01:05:00,780 --> 01:05:05,914
The documentation shows installation and GitHub-to-Bot account-linking steps.

166
01:05:37,284 --> 01:05:39,950
Follow the four steps to request access.

167
01:06:11,320 --> 01:06:15,320
Currently, only internal users have the approved login flow.

168
01:06:15,320 --> 01:06:20,454
One account can be used on two machines, and a team machine can also be used.

169
01:07:00,200 --> 01:07:05,200
Internet access is required and must be approved through the usual process.

170
01:07:05,200 --> 01:07:10,334
Use is allowed only for projects up to the applicable level 2 classification.

171
01:08:14,070 --> 01:08:16,137
Do not use it for SC3 projects.

172
01:09:19,873 --> 01:09:24,740
Confirm your project classification with the software PCM or team leader.

173
01:09:24,740 --> 01:09:29,274
A GitHub Copilot license costs roughly 40 euros per user each month.

174
01:10:10,992 --> 01:10:14,126
It is not intended for unrestricted deployment.

175
01:10:55,843 --> 01:11:00,910
Use approved individual or team-machine accounts through the proper process.

176
01:11:00,910 --> 01:11:02,977
Copilot can write code quickly.

177
01:11:38,162 --> 01:11:43,228
Users must understand the required behavior and review the output carefully.

178
01:12:18,413 --> 01:12:22,880
Leaders and reviewers should verify generated code before using it.

179
01:12:22,880 --> 01:12:25,147
Are there any remaining questions?

180
01:12:38,934 --> 01:12:40,534
Thank you for attending.

181
01:12:54,320 --> 01:12:55,520
Goodbye, everyone.
